@ 6.2.2.1.7 - Classical Arabic to Egyptian Arabic
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Brustad, Kristen, Mahmoud Al-Batal, and Abbas Al-Tonsi (2010), Alif Baa: Introduction to Letters and Sounds, 3rd. Ed.; http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_Arabic; At-Tonsi, Abbas, Heba Salem, and Nevenka Korica Sullivan (2013), Umm al-Dunya: Advanced Egyptian Colloquial Arabic; and from correspondence with my own Arabic professor, who is a native speaker of this dialect
	θ ð > t d
	θ ð > s z
	ðˤ > zˤ
	dˤ > zˤ
	dʒ > ɡ
	i u > e o / [- long] // _#
	u > {o,u} / _:[- long]#
	aj aw > e:[+ long] o:[+ long] / in U:[+closed]
	V:[+ long] > V / C_C{ː,C}V
	V > V:[+ long] / C_CV in U:[-stress]
	V > V:[+ long] / _# + suffix
	{i,u} > ∅ / VC_:[- long, -stress]CV
	# Some other short-vowel deletions
	∅ > e / CVCC_CVCV (applies across word boundaries)
	{ a, a:[+ long] } > ɑ(ː) / near emphatics
	{ a, a:[+ long] } > ɑ(ː) / if ɑ(ː) is elsewhere in the word
	{ a, a:[+ long] } > æ(ː) / else (sometimes it seems more like ɛ(ː) to me)
	q > ʔ
	# Two consecutive consonants assimilate to the voicing of the second (obstruents only?)
	{{s,z}(ˤ),ʒ}ʃ > ʃ:[+ long]
	ʕ > {ʕ̞,ħ} / _h
	# Final short vowel loss
	h > ∅ / in coda